1. IMPLEMENTED TRAINING MECHANISM

The implementation is Panda-inspired, with a DINO-style normalized projection
head, teacher-output centering/sharpening, and an exponential-moving-average
teacher. It is not a reproduction of either paper's complete recipe.

Teacher view:
  Complete measured event; no gradient; evaluation mode.

Student views:
  Two independent subsets of the same event. For each observed element type,
  retain max(1, floor(0.6 * number_of_elements)) uniformly without replacement.
  For raw hits the types are tracker and calorimeter. Sampling is not stratified
  by particle, layer, or ECAL/HCAL subdivision. Original row order is preserved.

Retained raw measurements are unchanged. Dropped rows are physically removed
before feature engineering and attention. Neighborhood features and HEPT groups
are recomputed using only retained rows. HEPT inputs are padded to a multiple of
128 with masking; padded rows do not enter the objective.

For each retained hit, the student predicts the full-event teacher's prototype
distribution at the corresponding original input row. No particle ownership is
used for correspondence. There is no prediction at removed-hit locations and no
explicit objective associating different hits from the same particle.

Defaults used in the hit runs:
  Prototype count 256; projection bottleneck 64; projection hidden width 256.
  Student temperature 0.1; teacher temperature 0.04.
  Teacher EMA momentum 0.996; center momentum 0.9.
  Loss averages hits within type, types within event, events, then student views.
  PF readouts remain frozen during SSL; periodic probes train copies of them.

With large hit counts, the two student views overlap on approximately 36% of
original hits and jointly cover approximately 84%. At least one hit per type
does not guarantee survival of any particular trajectory, shower, or layer.

