2. RAW-HIT DATA AND PERFORMANCE WORK

Dataset: cld_edm_ttbar_hits/1:3.2.1
Data root: /mnt/work/mlpf/tensorflow_datasets/cld
Dataset metadata: 90,000 training events and 10,000 official test events.
SSL/probe training uses train[:95%]; validation uses train[95%:]. The official
test split has not been used in these studies.

Model: pyg-cld-hits-heptv2-set-v1, shared six-layer HEPTv2 backbone, width 256,
with a 256-slot PF set decoder. Geometry, tracker-neighborhood, and calorimeter-
neighborhood features are enabled. Tracker doublets/momentum features are off.

Historical GPU tuning on the RTX 5060 Ti 16 GB:
  On the largest event in the original 512-event pool (13,446 hits), SSL batch 2
  reserved approximately 9.1 GiB and processed 4.10 events/s. Batch 3 bought less
  than 1% throughput for approximately 13.5 GiB reserved; batch 4 ran out of memory.
  Activation checkpointing allowed larger batches but reduced throughput.
  The selected recipe uses BF16, SSL batch 2, and no activation checkpointing.
  Probe batch 16 and hit-grouping chunk size 8192 reduced probe overhead.

Performance changes included prefetched input-only loading, vectorized view
sampling and balanced reductions, fused AdamW, batched EMA updates, CUDA/CPU
phase timings, and frozen embedding caches for probe fitting.

Matched multi-seed probe workers prepare shared embedding caches serially before
concurrent readout training. This resolved GPU memory failures during simultaneous
encoder extraction. Probe runs save optimizer/readout/RNG state at evaluations.

