3. HISTORICAL SMALL RAW-HIT RUN AND MATCHED PROBES

Historical run: experiments/cld_hits_ssl
SSL: 512 training events, 1,000 updates, batch 2.
Periodic probes: 64 training events, 16 validation events, 200 updates, batch 16.

The early analysis found expanding embedding covariance rank and no indication
of constant-vector collapse on the saved cohort. Falling SSL loss coincided with
sharpening teacher assignments; it did not reliably predict PF improvements.
Hit-pair probes showed no clear tracker association gain and only small,
uncertain calorimeter changes. Calorimeter pair prevalence was high, so absolute
average precision alone was misleading; geometry baselines remained important.

Completed longer matched campaign:
  SSL checkpoints 0, 500, 1000.
  Seeds 31415, 31416, 31417, 31418, 31419.
  2,000 probe updates each; 15 completed trials.
  Identical initial readouts and minibatch sequences across SSL checkpoints
  within each seed; frozen encoders; same 64/16 event split and optimizer.

Validation particle F1, mean +/- seed standard deviation:
  Probe updates     SSL 0           SSL 500         SSL 1000
  200               .289 +/- .017   .316 +/- .012   .297 +/- .020
  500               .332 +/- .044   .349 +/- .011   .349 +/- .011
  1000              .358 +/- .009   .346 +/- .013   .342 +/- .008
  2000              .347 +/- .009   .352 +/- .010   .338 +/- .010

At 200 updates, SSL 500 improved F1 in all seeds: paired mean +.0266, nominal
95% paired t interval [.0196, .0335]. At 2,000 updates the difference was +.0054
with interval [-.0145, .0252]. Intervals describe probe-seed variation on one
fixed event sample; they are not corrected for multiple comparisons.

At 2,000 updates, training F1 was .396/.467/.476 for SSL 0/500/1000, while
validation loss was 2.737/2.887/2.974. This indicated stronger training fits and
overfitting, without an established lasting validation advantage.

The replay differed from the original periodic probes. The matched campaign
therefore used its own initialized-backbone baseline; its results were not
stitched onto the earlier periodic curves. The numerical cause was not established.

Conclusion at that stage: increase the labeled probe sample as well as the SSL
data. Merely extending probes on 64 events was unlikely to resolve generalization.

4. COMPLETED LARGER RAW-HIT RUN

Run: experiments/cld_hits_ssl_large
SSL: 20,000 training events, 128 validation events, batch 2, 50,000 updates.
This is five passes' worth of sampled event exposure, not a guarantee of five
visits to every event. Learning rate .0001, weight decay .01, BF16.
Periodic PF probes: 1,024 training / 256 validation events, 1,000 updates,
batch 16, learning rate .001, weight decay .01, seed 31415.
Probes at initialization and every 10,000 SSL updates; hit-pair probes disabled.
Embedding snapshots every 1,000 steps, four fixed validation events, up to
2,048 saved hits/event after encoding each complete event.

Verified: all 50,000 training updates, finite training metrics and gradient norms,
six completed probes, and 51 finite snapshots with identical input-row identities.

SSL step   PF loss   Particle F1   Matched PID accuracy   Event-energy error*
0          2.4754    .3303         .6830                  .2208
10000      2.5244    .3594         .6764                  .2362
20000      2.3753    .3569         .6545                  .1779
30000      2.4160    .3557         .6905                  .1681
40000      2.3871    .3824         .6943                  .1342
50000      2.3808    .3746         .6890                  .1315
*Mean absolute relative error in total event energy.

Step 40,000 gives the best observed F1: +.0521 absolute, +15.8% relative to
initialization. Recall rises .2820 -> .3742; precision changes .3985 -> .3910.
Predicted multiplicity improves 67.34 -> 91.07 versus 95.16 targets/event.

At step 50,000, multiplicity drops to 74.72; precision increases to .4258 but
recall falls to .3343. The final checkpoint is not uniformly better than 40,000.
Event-energy absolute relative error improves by 39.2% at 40,000 and 40.4% at
50,000 relative to initialization. MET magnitude error changes only modestly:
18.28 -> 17.75 -> 18.00 GeV for steps 0/40000/50000.

PID gains are uneven. At 50,000, matched PID accuracy is .537 for charged
hadrons, .160 for neutral hadrons, .915 for photons, .161 for electrons, and
0 for muons. Charged-hadron PID was .670 at initialization. These are conditional
on kinematically matched populations, which change between checkpoints.
Particle F1 itself does not require correct PID.

Embedding evolution on the fixed 8,192-hit cohort (846 tracker, 7,346 calorimeter):
  Covariance entropy rank: all 10.02 -> 32.41; tracker 8.38 -> 18.48;
  calorimeter 5.74 -> 22.60.
  First-PC variance share: all 43.9% -> 10.2%.
  Final linear CKA to initialization: .291.
  Final student/teacher CKA: .995; CKA to previous 1,000-step snapshot: .990.
  Mean feature std: .457 -> 4.377; representation scale changes substantially.
  Cosine k=15 neighbor retention versus initialization: 34.5% on a fixed
  2,000-hit subsample. This is a structural diagnostic, not a semantic score.

Validation distillation loss is .0886 at 10,000 and .0950 at 50,000, and does not
rank the PF checkpoints reliably. Final teacher entropy is .0306 nats; effective
prototype count is 27.28. The latter averages batch-local marginal effective
counts and is not the number of globally active prototypes.

Training-only throughput averages 5.34 events/s over 5.20 hours. Elapsed time
from first update through final diagnostics is 6.16 hours, excluding initial
diagnostics. All six probes together take .82 hours. Peak logged training memory
is 9.40 GiB allocated / 11.11 GiB reserved.

Conclusion: promising fixed-budget transfer, particularly recall and event-energy
closure. Step 40,000 is a provisional reconstruction checkpoint; retain 50,000
for comparison. One SSL seed and one probe seed cannot establish convergence or
seed robustness. Different samples/budgets prevent a controlled numerical
comparison with the earlier small run. Longer multi-seed probes on this large
run have been recommended but have not been run in this session.

