5. AUGMENTATION ASSESSMENT

Hit thinning is more defensible than deleting whole tracks/clusters because a
trajectory or shower may have redundant measurements. Recomputing neighborhood
features makes this a real context perturbation. However:
  - Uniform 40% removal is an aggressive, uncalibrated detector corruption.
  - Sparse trajectories and dense showers have different redundancy.
  - Expected retained calorimeter hit-energy sum is approximately 60% of full.
  - All retained-hit geometry and measurements remain unchanged, allowing an
    individual-hit predictor to solve much of the prototype task.
  - The projection output is constrained; the whole backbone embedding need
    not become invariant. PF information can remain outside the projection.

Proposed, not implemented: milder modality-specific thinning; bounded structured
surface/region losses; detector-calibrated measurement perturbations; context-
dependent prediction with masked measurement groups. Derived/redundant features
must be updated to prevent information leakage or inconsistent measurements.
Changing thinning strength alone does not remove the individual-hit shortcut.

6. COMPLETED CONTEXT AND INDIVIDUAL-HIT DIAGNOSTICS

Run: experiments/cld_hits_ssl_large/diagnostics
Checkpoint: final step 50,000, from latest.pth.
All data access is input-only. No particle labels were used.

Context protocol:
  64 validation events, 16 query hits/type/event, chosen among survivors of the
  actual 60% view sampler. Query measurements are identical in every condition.
  Evaluate initialized encoder, final teacher, and final student.
  Conditions: full repeat; 60% retained; foreign context; query-only; isolated.
  Foreign context preserves event length, types, query positions, and query
  features. Four other events supply donor rows; no recycling was required.
  Query-only events contain 32 query hits. Isolated hits are separate one-hit
  events. These are extreme stress tests, not realistic detector models.
  Initial-reference comparisons cover embeddings only: the original SSL
  projection head was not saved with the initial PF probe checkpoint.

Teacher prototype agreement with its own full-event targets:
Condition           Tracker, 95% event-bootstrap CI     Calorimeter, CI
Full repeat         100%                               100%
60% retained        96.97% [95.90,97.85]                 99.02% [98.44,99.51]
Foreign context     83.69% [81.25,86.04]                 93.46% [91.70,95.12]
Queries only        81.93% [79.30,84.57]                 71.39% [67.97,74.90]
Isolated hits       71.00% [68.16,73.63]                 34.77% [30.57,38.87]

Student agreement with the full teacher is similar: 96.29%/98.14% under thinning
and 84.38%/92.58% under foreign context (tracker/calorimeter).
Teacher relative embedding L2 changes are 9.75%/10.65% under thinning and
35.67%/38.07% under foreign context. Full-repeat numerical differences are about
.1-.2%; prototype argmax stays identical.

Relative to within-type query embedding spread, thinning changes shrink from
.192/.260 for the initialized encoder to .106/.118 for the teacher. Context
sensitivity exists even at initialization, so it alone is not evidence of learned
particle association. Bootstrap intervals condition on one SSL checkpoint.

Individual-hit predictor protocol:
  256 training events, 128 disjoint validation events, up to 256 hits/type/event.
  Three seeds (8123,8124,8125), each fitted for 4,000 updates.
  MLP: two 256-wide GELU hidden layers; soft full-event teacher targets.
  Training-only standardization; equal event/type weighting.
  Geometry input: position/direction and detector metadata; excludes energy,
  transverse energy, and time. All-feature input: all 15 raw columns.
  Neither predictor receives neighborhood features or other hits.

Held-out prototype agreement, mean across three seeds:
Predictor                        Tracker    Calorimeter    Equal-type average
Detector-type marginal           12.52%     10.64%         11.58%
Geometry-only MLP                86.06%     93.54%         89.80%
All-raw-feature MLP              87.49%     93.59%         90.54%

Equal-type seed std is .031 percentage points for geometry-only and .129 for
all-feature predictors. These do not quantify event-sampling uncertainty.
Held-out KL is approximately .220 nats for geometry-only and .203 for all
features, versus 2.865 for the detector-type marginal baseline.

Interpretation:
  The teacher's prototype choices are largely predictable from individual-hit
  geometry and detector identity. Additional energy/time measurements contribute
  little to aggregate agreement, especially in calorimetry. Thinning leaves the
  predictive individual-hit features unchanged.

  The actual encoder nevertheless uses context. Poor isolated-model behavior
  does not contradict the pointwise predictor: isolated events are outside the
  encoder's usual neighborhood distribution, while the separate predictor is
  explicitly trained to predict full-event targets from individual-hit inputs.

  These tests establish target predictability and network context sensitivity.
  They do not prove that all embedding information is geometric, that the PF
  gains are spurious, or that prototype assignments correspond to particles.
  Geometry-only also mixes absolute position and useful detector/layer metadata;
  separating those requires further feature ablations.

