7. NEXT EXPERIMENTS — PROPOSED, NOT YET EXECUTED

1. Longer matched PF learning curves on the large-run checkpoints 0/40000/50000,
   using five seeds and the same 1,024/256 event sample. This tests whether the
   observed PF advantage persists after longer readout optimization.
2. Restore hit-pair association probes to distinguish geometry encoding from
   track/shower grouping improvements. Retain raw-feature baselines and account
   for positive-pair prevalence.
3. Design a context-dependent target that cannot largely be recovered from the
   query hit's unchanged geometry. Masking must cover redundant/derived fields.
4. Keep the present augmentation as a control; compare milder and structured
   corruptions at matched data/compute budgets, followed by the same diagnostics.
5. Separate position-only, detector/layer-only, and combined individual-hit
   predictors if identifying the specific shortcut is needed before redesign.

