12. REGION SSL BENEFIT AS A FUNCTION OF SSL UPDATES — 2026-11-16

Computed from existing matched five-seed probes; no new training. Hold readout
training budget fixed and compare each checkpoint against the initialized encoder
with an equally trained readout, paired by probe seed. Use unweighted_task_loss:
the fixed sum of binary classification, PID classification, pT/eta/sin(phi)/
cos(phi)/energy losses. It excludes Cardinality, HitGrouping and Auxiliary.
Calibrated Total weights can differ across readouts, making Total a less direct
representation-quality comparison. Component sums were checked numerically.

At 5,000 readout updates (mean +/- sample SD across five probe seeds):
  SSL updates    PF task loss        Reduction versus SSL 0    Particle F1
  0              2.18852 +/- .02811  0                         .37252
  60,000         1.87455 +/- .01556  .31397 (14.35%)            .44490
  80,000         1.80068 +/- .02192  .38784 (17.72%)            .45512
  100,000        1.78237 +/- .04718  .40615 (18.56%)            .46211
Percent reductions divide mean paired reduction by mean baseline loss.

Incremental PF loss reduction, paired by seed:
  0 -> 60k:   .31397 +/- .02395; .05233 per 10k SSL updates, 5/5 improve.
  60k -> 80k: .07387 +/- .03044; .03694 per 10k SSL updates, 5/5 improve.
  80k -> 100k:.01831 +/- .04634; .00915 per 10k SSL updates, 4/5 improve.
The last interval has a small mean improvement relative to seed variation;
these SDs are not confidence intervals. No claim of a confirmed saturation point.
60k captures 77.3% and 80k captures 95.5% of the observed 0->100k loss reduction.
80k is therefore a reasonable compute-saving candidate, while 100k has the best
sampled mean PF loss/F1 under the longest evaluated readout budget.

The shape depends on readout budget. At 1,000 readout updates, PF loss means
for SSL 0/60k/80k/100k are 2.37763/2.09288/2.01806/2.03536. At 2,000 updates
they are 2.35486/1.96536/1.90425/1.88553. A short probe could misleadingly suggest
that 80k is better than 100k; hold the probe budget fixed and inspect several
budgets. The 5,000-update probe itself is not demonstrably fully converged.

At 100k versus SSL 0, absolute base loss reductions are .11265 PID classification,
.11048 pT regression, .11012 energy regression, .07064 binary classification,
and .00226 combined angular regressions. PID/pT/energy account for 82.1% of the
fixed-sum reduction. This describes contributions to this scalar loss, not a
unit-independent physical importance ranking. HitGrouping improves .04382 but
is excluded from the quoted base-task sum. Calibrated Total decreases overall
3.88383 -> 3.67228, but increases slightly from 80k to 100k; do not conflate
that calibrated objective with the fixed unweighted comparison above.

Only SSL 0/60k/80k/100k have matched multiseed long probes. Earlier periodic
20k/40k probes have a different budget/seed design and were not merged into
these curves. Early-time scaling needs matched probes at those checkpoints.
Lines connect observed checkpoints, not a fitted scaling law. All results remain
conditional on this SSL trajectory and reused validation sample.

